INT. CAR WASH TUNNEL - NIGHT

A beat-up hatchback inches through the dark. Foam slides down the windshield in thick white curtains. Brushes thump and whirl.

SOLEDAD, 18, sits in the driver's seat in her graduation gown, cap still on. KWAME, 18, in the passenger seat, holds a diploma cover on his lap.

She stares ahead. He stares at the foam.

The car stops in a pool of blue light. Water drums on the roof.

KWAME
We could've gone to the party.

SOLEDAD
You hate parties.

KWAME
I hate that one.

She nods. Her hands tighten on the wheel. The brushes retreat. A jet of water hits the glass, and for a moment the world outside is nothing but a trembling white.

SOLEDAD
You got the letter.

KWAME
Yeah.

SOLEDAD
Where?

KWAME
Out west.

She says nothing. He turns the diploma cover over in his hands. She puts the car in drive, and it rolls forward into the dryers, which roar.

He rolls his window down and lets the warm air in. She takes off her cap and places it on the dash.
